---

<DocPage
  title="useAction"
  eyebrow="Primitive"
  path="components/use-action"
  meta={meta}
  lede="One hook owns the lifecycle of one asynchronous action: the synchronous duplicate guard, the pending affordance that neither flashes nor flickers, abort on unmount, classified failures, automatic retry with an observable wait, and the announcement a screen-reader user hears."
  description="useAction reference: the six-state action machine, resource keys, retry, cancellation, the full options and return tables, accessibility and keyboard behaviour."
>
  <Section id="description" title="Description">
    <p>
      <code>useAction</code> takes the work as its first argument and an options object as its second. The work
      receives the trigger arguments and an <code>AbortSignal</code>, and it is replaced on every committed
      render, so reading fresh state inside it is safe.
    </p>

    <p>
      Underneath is a six-state machine. Four of those states are what a user can distinguish, which is what <code
        >status</code
      > reports. The other two exist for timing, and they are the reason this is a machine rather than a boolean.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>idle</code></strong> — before the first trigger, and again after <code>reset()</code>,
        after a <code>resetAfterMs</code> window elapses, after <code>cancel()</code>, and after an aborted
        request. An abort is our own cancellation, not a failure, so it does not surface here as an error.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>busy</code></strong> — entered on a trigger that the guard allows. The request is an effect
        of this state, so its lifetime is exactly this state. Whether the pending affordance is visible yet is context
        driven by the delay timer, not a separate state.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>settling</code></strong> — entered when the request settles while the affordance has been
        visible for less than <code>pending.min</code>. The outcome is already known and is held here until
        the minimum has elapsed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>backoff</code></strong> — entered when a retryable failure arrives and the retry budget is
        not exhausted. The machine waits here for the computed delay, then retries.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>success</code></strong> — entered on resolve when no hold is needed, or from
        <code>settling</code> with a stashed success. On entry it announces politely and calls
        <code>onSuccess</code> then <code>onSettled</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>error</code></strong> — entered on reject when no hold and no retry apply, from
        <code>settling</code> with a stashed failure, or from <code>backoff</code> when the pending retry is cancelled.
        On entry it announces assertively and calls <code>onError</code> then
        <code>onSettled</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why settling and backoff are states</h3>

    <p>
      <code>settling</code> exists so a fast action never flashes and a shown affordance never flickers. Those are
      two different thresholds. The 150 ms delay means a 60 ms save shows nothing at all. The 400 ms minimum means
      a save that resolves at 160 ms, just after the affordance appeared, does not produce a 10 ms flicker: the
      outcome waits in <code>settling</code> until the affordance has been on screen long enough to be read. A single
      boolean cannot express both thresholds, and splitting visibility into its own state would double the state
      count of every machine that composes this one.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>backoff</code> exists so a waiting retry is observable rather than a hang. Retrying inside the data
      layer looks identical to a stuck request from the outside: the button is pending, nothing is happening, and
      the user has no way to tell whether to wait or to press again. Because the wait is a state with a <code
        >retryAt</code
      > reading on it, the UI can render <em>retrying in 3s</em> and the user can decline it with <code
        >cancel()</code
      >.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>busy</code>, <code>settling</code> and <code>backoff</code> all report
      <code>status: "pending"</code>, so simple call sites never see the distinction. Read
      <code>state</code> when you need it.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="demo" title="Live demo">
    <p>Both of these are real calls into the library. Nothing here is mocked and no outcome is hard-coded.</p>

    <Demo
      title="One button, one declaration"
      hint="900 ms request"
      note="Notice three things: the label changes instead of the button disappearing, the control keeps focus throughout, and the result stays on screen afterwards."
    >
      <AsyncButtonDemo client:load latencyMs={900} label="Save customer" />
    </Demo>

    <Demo
      title="Where the thresholds fall"
      hint="700 ms request · delay 150 · min 400"
      note="The affordance appears at 150 ms, the request resolves at 700 ms, and the affordance stays until it has been visible for 400 ms."
    >
      <TimingTrace client:visible latencyMs={700} windowMs={1200} />
    </Demo>
  </Section>

  <Section id="installation" title="Installation">
    <Snippet code={installCode} lang="bash" name="shell" />

    <p>
      <code>@unruffled/react</code> depends on <code>@unruffled/core</code> and on nothing else. Both packages have
      zero runtime dependencies. React is a peer dependency, <code>^18</code> or
      <code>^19</code>. The React layer is 2.07 kB brotli; the React and core layers together, with everything
      retained, are 7.08 kB brotli.
    </p>

    <p>
      One further step, and it is the one that matters: render <code>Announcer</code> once near the root. Announcement
      is on by default, and mounting the live region is all that is required. Without it, a pending state that outlasts
      400 ms reports{" "}
      <a href={href("diagnostics/ux1003")}>UX1003</a>, because the library can tell the difference between
      announcing correctly and announcing into a void.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={rootCode} name="src/App.tsx" />

    <Callout tone="note" title="The provider is optional">
      <p>
        Every hook works without <code>UnruffledProvider</code>. A library that only behaves correctly once a
        provider is wired has made its first-run experience the thing most likely to be got wrong. Add it when
        you want one timing policy, one failure taxonomy or one root for the whole application.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="basic-usage" title="Basic usage">
    <Snippet code={basicCode} name="src/customers/SaveCustomer.tsx" />

    <p>
      That is the whole call site. The second click is refused synchronously, the spinner does not appear for
      a fast save and does not flicker for a slow one, the request aborts if the component unmounts, the
      failure is classified and only offered for retry when retrying could work, and both outcomes are
      announced.
    </p>

    <h3>pending and busy are different</h3>

    <p>
      This is the one distinction worth learning before anything else. <code>busy</code> is true from the first
      millisecond of the request. <code>pending</code> is whether the affordance should render.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={pendingVsBusyCode} name="pending-vs-busy.tsx" />

    <p>
      Spread <code>triggerProps</code> and you get the correct attributes without reading either flag: the element
      carries <code>aria-busy</code> from <code>busy</code> and <code>data-pending</code> from
      <code>pending</code>. Read the booleans only for content, such as a label or a spinner element.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="advanced-usage" title="Advanced usage">
    <h3>Resource keys and cross-component contention</h3>

    <p>
      A boolean on one hook cannot express the case that actually breaks. Two buttons in two different
      subtrees that write the same record have to contend, and neither one knows the other exists. So the
      guard is not a flag on the hook; it is a registry keyed by resource identity, written synchronously
      inside the event handler, and shared by every hook that declares the same key.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={keyCode} name="src/invoices/approve.tsx" />

    <p>
      Both of those contend. Press either and the other is refused until the request settles. The key is a
      string you choose, and it should be derived from the resource, not from the component.
    </p>

    <Demo
      title="Two components, one record"
      hint="Toggle the shared key"
      note="Press one, then immediately press the other. With a key, the second is refused. Without one, both writes land and the value the user ends up with depends on which response arrives last."
    >
      <GuardDemo client:visible />
    </Demo>

    <p>
      An overlapping trigger on an action with no key cannot be judged, because the library has no way to know
      whether the two triggers touch the same resource. It is reported as{" "}
      <a href={href("diagnostics/ux1002")}>UX1002</a> rather than silently allowed or silently blocked.
    </p>

    <p>
      The registry is process-wide by default, because the contention it models is genuinely global. Pass your
      own when you need isolation, which is mostly tests.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={registryCode} name="src/test/registry.ts" />

    <p>When overlapping work is additive rather than duplicated, say so explicitly.</p>

    <Snippet code={concurrentCode} name="allow-concurrent.tsx" />

    <h3>Automatic retry</h3>

    <p>
      A retry policy only retries failures whose <code>canRetry</code> is true. A 403 is never retried, and neither
      is a 422: both would be a pointless round trip and a false promise to the user. The delay comes from exponential
      backoff with full jitter at 0.5 by default, because without jitter every client that failed at the same moment
      retries at the same moment, which is how a brief outage becomes a sustained one.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={retryCode} name="src/customers/save-with-retry.tsx" />

    <Demo
      title="Retry with a live countdown"
      hint="Fails twice, then succeeds"
      note="The state goes busy, backoff, busy, backoff, busy, success. The countdown is a real value derived from retryAt through the frame port."
    >
      <RetryDemo client:visible />
    </Demo>

    <p>
      With no retry policy, nothing retries by itself. Offer the affordance yourself, and use
      <code>canRetry</code> to decide whether offering it is honest.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={manualRetryCode} name="manual-retry.tsx" />

    <h3>Parameterised actions</h3>

    <p>
      When one hook serves many triggers, pass the argument on the trigger rather than creating a hook per
      row. <code>getTriggerProps</code> composes an <code>args</code> override, and your own
      <code>onClick</code>, <code>onKeyDown</code> and <code>ref</code> alongside it. Your handlers run after the
      library's and only when the activation was not suppressed, so an analytics call does not fire on a refused
      duplicate click.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={argsCode} name="src/cart/LineActions.tsx" />

    <p>
      The arguments of the current attempt are kept, so <code>retry()</code> re-runs the attempt that failed with
      the arguments it was given. <code>TriggerPropOverrides</code> types
      <code>disabled</code> as <code>never</code>: it is not an oversight, and the reason is under
      <a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a> below.
    </p>

    <h3>Cancellation and abort</h3>

    <p>
      The request is declared as an effect of the <code>busy</code> state, so leaving <code>busy</code> for any
      reason disposes it, and disposal aborts. That single fact covers three cases that are normally handled separately
      or not at all:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Unmount.</strong> The service stops, the effect is disposed, the request aborts. Nothing resolves
        into a dead component. Strict Mode's deliberate mount, unmount, remount is survived without tearing down
        the machine, because the stop is deferred by a microtask and cancelled if a mount follows immediately.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Supersession.</strong> A new attempt disposes the previous effect, which aborts the stale request
        with the reason <code>superseded</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>cancel()</code>.</strong> Aborts with the reason <code>cancelled</code> and returns to idle.
        The resulting rejection is recognised as an abort, so it is not classified as a failure, not retried and
        not announced. Calling <code>cancel()</code> from <code>backoff</code> instead commits the failure, which
        is how a user declines a pending retry.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <Snippet code={cancelCode} name="src/files/Upload.tsx" />

    <p>
      Machines never call <code>fetch</code>. The work is handed to the transport port, which supplies the
      signal. That is why unmounting mid-request, superseding a stale attempt and cancelling by hand all work
      by the same mechanism rather than three.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="api" title="API">
    <p>
      <code>useAction(run, options?)</code>. Three tables: what goes in, what comes back, and what
      <code>triggerProps</code> puts on the element. The options type is everything
      <code>ActionProps</code> accepts except the work itself, plus the port overrides.
    </p>

    <h3>Options</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={optionRows} caption="useAction options" />

    <h3>Returns</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={returnRows} nameHeading="Value" caption="useAction return value" />

    <h3>triggerProps</h3>
    <PropsTable
      rows={triggerRows}
      nameHeading="Attribute"
      caption="Attributes and handlers spread by triggerProps"
    />

    <Callout tone="note" title="There is no disabled">
      <p>
        <code>TriggerProps</code> has no <code>disabled</code> field and
        <code>TriggerPropOverrides</code> types it as <code>never</code>. This is the most consequential
        decision in the hook, and the next section is why.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="accessibility" title="Accessibility">
    <h3>aria-busy and aria-disabled, not disabled</h3>

    <p>
      A <code>disabled</code> element is removed from the tab order. Disabling a button at the moment it is pressed
      therefore throws the user's focus to the top of the document and loses their place in the page. For a keyboard
      user that is a navigation reset in the middle of their own action; for a screen reader user the control they
      were on simply ceases to exist, with no explanation.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the trigger stays focusable. <code>aria-busy</code> is true while work is in flight, settling or waiting
      to retry. <code>aria-disabled</code> is true while activation is suppressed, and is omitted rather than rendered
      as <code>false</code>, because <code>aria-disabled="false"</code> is noise in the accessibility tree. Activation
      is refused in the handlers instead: the click handler returns early, and the keydown handler prevents the
      default for Enter and Space. The second press is blocked, the state is announced, and focus is preserved.
    </p>

    <p>
      Adding <code>disabled</code> by hand to a control that has a reason for being unavailable is the defect{
        " "
      }
      <a href={href("diagnostics/ux1005")}>UX1005</a> describes. The type catches it when it goes through
      <code>getTriggerProps</code>, where <code>disabled</code> is <code>never</code>; written straight onto
      your own element it still compiles. UX1005 is one of the four statically decidable rules and belongs to
      <code>@unruffled/eslint-plugin</code>, which has not shipped, so nothing reports it at runtime in 0.1.0.
    </p>

    <h3>Focus retention</h3>

    <p>
      Normally there is nothing to restore, because the trigger is never disabled and so never loses focus.
      The case the hook does handle is the one that bites in optimistic lists: the surrounding subtree is
      replaced while the action is in flight, which leaves focus on the document body. On the transition from
      pending to settled, if the owning root reports no active element, focus returns to the trigger with <code
        >preventScroll</code
      >. Set <code>focus: "none"</code> for actions triggered without a pointer or a key press, where moving focus
      would be an interruption.
    </p>

    <h3>Announcements</h3>

    <p>
      Announcement is on by default and silence is the opt-out. With no <code>announce</code> option at all, success
      announces <em>Done.</em> politely and failure announces the normalised failure message assertively. Failures
      interrupt on purpose: a user who has moved on still needs to know the save failed. The pending string has
      no fallback, so nothing is announced when work starts unless you supply <code>announce.pending</code>;
      an announcement on every click would be noise.
    </p>

    <p>
      All three strings are overridable, and <code>announce: false</code> turns the lot off. Reach for that only
      when another live region is already reporting the same outcome, and see the mistakes below.
    </p>

    <h3>The rules this hook reports</h3>

    <p>
      Every rule below fires when the wiring is wrong, never when an accessibility feature is enabled. They
      are development-only: <code>__DEV__</code> is replaced with <code>false</code> in the default build, so the
      rule table, the formatter and every call site collapse to nothing and the production bundle carries no diagnostics
      bytes.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><a href={href("diagnostics/ux1001")}>UX1001</a></strong> — reported on the first trigger when <code
          >destructive</code
        > is true and <code>reversible</code> is not. An irreversible action can be fired by one mis-click or a
        stray Enter on a focused control, and the user has no way back.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href={href("diagnostics/ux1002")}>UX1002</a></strong> — reported when a second trigger arrives
        while the action is busy and no key was given. Two overlapping writes to the same resource land in nondeterministic
        order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href={href("diagnostics/ux1003")}>UX1003</a></strong> — reported at settle time when the pending
        affordance was visible for 400 ms or more and no live region was mounted. Checked on the evidence that the
        wait actually happened, not on the possibility that it might. A sighted user had clear feedback for that
        whole period and a screen reader user had none.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href={href("diagnostics/ux1004")}>UX1004</a></strong> — reported on entry to
        <code>error</code> when there is no <code>onError</code>. The rejection reaches the console but not
        the user, so a failed save looks identical to a successful one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section id="keyboard" title="Keyboard">
    <Keys rows={keyRows} />

    <p>
      Keyboard activation is synthesised only for a non-button element. A native
      <code>button</code> already turns Enter and Space into a click, so firing the action from the keydown handler
      as well would run it twice. The handler checks the tag name and only synthesises activation when the element
      is not a <code>BUTTON</code>.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={nonButtonCode} name="non-button-trigger.tsx" />

    <Callout tone="warning" title="Prefer a real button">
      <p>
        The non-button path exists because design systems ship these, not because it is a good idea. A native
        button brings activation, form participation, the correct default role and platform shortcuts you will
        otherwise reimplement one at a time.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="customization" title="Customization">
    <h3>Styling</h3>

    <p>
      The library emits no CSS, no class names and no DOM of its own. The trigger carries the lifecycle as
      attributes, so the visual is entirely yours and there is nothing to override.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={stylingCode} lang="css" name="src/styles/action.css" />

    <h3>Timing and copy in one place</h3>

    <p>
      Setting the pending policy per call site is how inconsistency gets into a product. The provider holds
      the defaults, and a hook's own options are merged on top of them. The provider's defaults are
      behavioural only: there is nothing about colour, radius, spacing or typography, because the library has
      no visual and so has no opinion to configure.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={providerCode} name="src/main.tsx" />

    <h3>Classification</h3>

    <p>
      <code>classify</code> replaces the status mapping for the cases you care about and falls through to the default
      for everything else, so one special case does not mean restating the table.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={classifyCode} name="src/api/classify.ts" />

    <h3>Ports</h3>

    <p>
      Every impure capability is injected: clock, scheduler, storage, navigation, doc, announcer, transport
      and logger. Pass <code>ports</code> to replace any of them, and <code>root</code> to scope listeners and focus
      reads to a shadow root or a secondary window. The clock is the one most often replaced, because it is what
      makes a 150 ms threshold assertable without sleeping.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={testCode} name="src/customers/save.test.tsx" />

    <p>There are 50 tests in the repository and none of them sleep.</p>
  </Section>

