  <Section id="mistakes" title="Common mistakes">
    <Mistake
      title="Adding disabled to the trigger"
      why="A disabled element leaves the tab order. The user pressed the button, and the browser's answer is to move their focus to the top of the document. The trigger is already aria-disabled and already refuses the second press, so the attribute adds nothing but the regression. TriggerProps never carries a disabled key, and TriggerPropOverrides types it as never so getTriggerProps rejects one; written directly on your own element, as below, it still compiles. That is the case UX1005 describes, and the ESLint plugin that will catch it has not shipped yet."
      bad={`<button {...save.triggerProps} disabled={save.pending}>
  {save.pending ? "Saving" : "Save"}
</button>`}
      good={`// The spread already carries aria-busy and aria-disabled.
// Change the label, not the tab order.
<button {...save.triggerProps}>
  {save.pending ? "Saving" : "Save"}
</button>`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Omitting the resource key"
      why="Without a key the guard has nothing to serialise on. A second trigger from a different component writes the same record, the two responses land in whatever order the network chooses, and the value the user ends up with depends on timing. Keying by component instead of by resource has the same effect, because two components can write one record."
      bad={`// Toolbar and row both save invoice 7. Neither knows the other exists.
const approve = useAction(() => api.approve(id));`}
      good={`// Derived from the resource, so every hook touching it contends.
const approve = useAction(() => api.approve(id), {
  key: \`invoice:\${id}\`,
});`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Reading pending to decide whether to call trigger"
      why="pending is a rendered value, so it is at least one render behind the press. Two clicks inside that window both see false and both pass the check. The real guard is synchronous and inside the machine: it reads the registry in the event handler, before any await and before any validation. Calling trigger unconditionally is correct, because a duplicate is refused at the point it is made."
      bad={`<button
  onClick={() => {
    if (!save.pending) save.trigger();
  }}
>
  Save
</button>`}
      good={`// Refused synchronously when the key is held. No flag to read.
<button {...save.triggerProps}>Save</button>

// For a non-click trigger, the same applies:
<form onSubmit={(event) => { event.preventDefault(); save.trigger(); }}>`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title='Setting announce: false because a toast "already says it"'
      why="A toast is not a live region unless the toast library made it one, and most render into a portal with no aria-live at all. Turning the announcement off on that assumption leaves a screen reader user with no feedback that the action started, finished or failed. If the toast is genuinely announced, you have a different problem: the same message twice. Override the string to something shorter instead of silencing the channel."
      bad={`const save = useAction(run, {
  key: "customer:42",
  announce: false, // the toast says "Saved"
  onSuccess: () => toast("Saved"),
});`}
      good={`// One announcement, worded for the ear rather than the eye.
const save = useAction(run, {
  key: "customer:42",
  announce: { success: "Customer saved" },
  onSuccess: () => toast("Saved"),
});`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Retrying a failure that cannot succeed"
      why="A retry button on a 403 or a 422 is a lie: the same request will fail the same way, and the user presses it twice before concluding the product is broken. canRetry is false for aborted, auth, forbidden, missing, validation and terminal, and an automatic retry policy already honours it. Branch on it in your own UI too."
      bad={`{save.status === "error" ? (
  <button onClick={save.retry}>Try again</button>
) : null}`}
      good={`{save.status === "error" ? (
  <>
    <p>{save.failure?.message}</p>
    {save.canRetry ? <button onClick={save.retry}>Try again</button> : null}
  </>
) : null}`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Shipping without Announcer mounted"
      why="Announcement is on by default, but the default announcer port is inert until the live region mounts. Every announcement the hook makes goes nowhere, and nothing in the visual UI tells you. UX1003 reports it at settle time, with the elapsed pending duration, once a wait has actually outlasted 400 ms."
      bad={`// Announcements are configured and never heard.
createRoot(el).render(<App />);`}
      good={`// One instance, once, near the root.
createRoot(el).render(
  <>
    <App />
    <Announcer />
  </>,
);`}
    />
  </Section>
